-- title:
--   The half-open cubes over a scaled lattice tile Euclidean space
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$ and a real number $h > 0$. Let
--
--   $$h\mathbb{Z}^d = \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \text{every coordinate } x_i \text{ is an integer multiple of } h \,\}$$
--
--   be the scaled lattice, and for $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$ let
--
--   $$\tilde{A}_h(u) = \prod_{i=1}^{d} \bigl[\, u_i - \tfrac{h}{2},\; u_i + \tfrac{h}{2} \,\bigr)$$
--
--   be the half-open cube of side $h$ centred at $u$ (closed on the left, open on the right in each coordinate). Then for every point $s \in \mathbb{R}^d$ there is **exactly one** lattice point $x \in h\mathbb{Z}^d$ with $s \in \tilde{A}_h(x)$:
--
--   $$\forall\, s \in \mathbb{R}^d,\quad \exists!\, x \in h\mathbb{Z}^d \ \text{ such that } \ s \in \tilde{A}_h(x).$$
--
--   Equivalently, the cubes $\{\tilde{A}_h(x)\}_{x \in h\mathbb{Z}^d}$ partition $\mathbb{R}^d$.
--
--   Half-open cubes are used in preference to open or closed ones precisely so that this exact-tiling statement holds: every point of space is assigned to one and only one cube, which turns "the cube containing $s$" into a well-defined function of $s$ and lets a decomposition of $\mathbb{R}^d$ into cubes be summed over without double counting or gaps.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The lattice condition is stated coordinatewise ("for each $i$ there is an integer $n$ with $x_i = n h$") rather than as membership in an additive subgroup, and the ambient space is `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`, so coordinates are the components of the $\ell^2$ product. The uniqueness is Lean's `∃!` applied to the conjunction of lattice membership and cube membership.
